  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday morning through next Thursday)\nIssued at 145 PM EST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nAn upper level system will eject from of the Plains on Sunday, \npushing a front toward our area during the day. Ahead of this \nfrontal boundary, upper level diffluence will translate across the\nregion, providing sufficient lift for dynamically forced \nconvective development and the sustainment of a broken line of \nshowers and isolated embedded thunderstorms from Sunday afternoon \ninto Sunday night. Additionally, warm air advection (WAA) and a \nplume of Gulf moisture will overspread the region, increasing \nPWATs to 1-1.5 inches and pushing surface temperatures into the \nlower to upper 60s ahead of the frontal passage. As a result, SPC \nhas outlined a general thunder risk for the Day 3 outlook, with ML\nguidance hinting at a limited severe risk in a low CAPE, high \nshear environment, particularly over our western to northwestern \nGeorgia counties. Either way, we will continue to monitor the \nevolution of this system, as it does not take much added \ninstability to generate severe weather threats this time of year. \n\nFollowing the frontal passage, Monday will transition back to a \ndrier pattern with seasonable temperatures and clearing skies. \nSurface winds will be out of the northwest at 5-10 mph, followed \nby light winds and strong radiational cooling by Tuesday morning \nwith low temperatures ranging from the low 30s to low 40s across \nthe area. Surface winds will then shift to the northeast on \nTuesday as high pressure sets up over the Ohio River Valley, \nbriefly reestablishing the wedge over the area with a gradual \nwarming trend through the second half of next week.\n\nOur next chance of measurable rainfall will not arrive until \nThursday afternoon at the earliest, as another upper level low \nshifts out of the Plains. Guidance has not yet resolved the exact \nplacement of any organized system associated with this feature, so\nthe timing of local impacts is still developing. However, expect \nthe chance of another round of rainfall by the end of next week, \npossibly lingering into the weekend.",
